WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.030
By now, we’ve all heard stories about shops
selling toilet paper for 10 bucks a roll,

00:00:05.030 --> 00:00:10.150
or $70 bottles of hand sanitizer on
ebay, and we just boil with outrage.

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.910
Is there anything lower than price gouging
during a crisis?

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:17.920
I mean, what kind of a monster sees people
desperate and fearful and thinks, “Hey,

00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:19.800
I can make a killing off this!”

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.610
But, you might be surprised to know that there
is one group of people who actually think

00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.320
it’s not such a bad thing.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:32.000
No, it’s not the gougers themselves… it’s
economists.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:45.860
In 2012, the IGM Forum conducted a poll of
over 50 respected economic experts about a

00:00:45.870 --> 00:00:51.449
proposed Connecticut law against “price
gouging,” and found that only 7% agreed

00:00:51.449 --> 00:00:56.309
with it--which is surprising, considering
that over two-thirds of states have such laws

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:57.559
on the books.

00:00:57.559 --> 00:01:02.519
The wording varies from state to state, but
“gouging” is typically defined as raising

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:08.250
prices on essential goods during a disaster
or shortage to a level that’s “unfair”,

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:10.409
“excessive” or “unreasonable.”

00:01:10.409 --> 00:01:14.840
You might already see why people who deal
with numbers all day might have a problem

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:15.840
with this.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:18.560
How do you quantify “unreasonable”?

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.930
What amounts to “unfair”?

00:01:20.930 --> 00:01:29.020
Most people would find a 200% increase “excessive,”
but some states will punish even a 10-20% hike.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:33.200
State attorneys general across the
country are swamped with complaints about

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.740
COVID-related price gouging, and in the last
couple weeks, some charges are starting to

00:01:37.740 --> 00:01:38.750
be filed.

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.950
By the end of March, Amazon had removed over
half a million listings and suspended almost

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.420
4,000 sellers for “violating [their] fair
pricing policies.”

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:52.420
But many economists claim that this reaction
does more harm than good.

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:57.969
Prices, they say, are a way of allocating
resources to the places where they are valued most,

00:01:57.969 --> 00:02:03.650
and that becomes even more important
when resources are scarce, like during shortages,

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:06.120
natural disasters, and pandemics.

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:10.370
Imagine that you’re out of toilet paper,
so you put on your face mask and rush down

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:14.550
to your corner market and find out that (phew!)
they still have a few packs left.

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:18.540
But they’ve raised the price from $2.99
to $6.99!

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:23.110
Even though that’s more than double what
you’re used to paying, you really need that

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:27.480
TP, so you fork over the money and storm home,
certain that you got hosed.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:31.860
(Speaking of getting hosed, if you had a bidet
you wouldn’t be in this predicament.)

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.140
But! says the economist, what you didn’t
know was that earlier today your neighbor

00:02:36.140 --> 00:02:38.160
Ted visited the same store.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.860
Ted’s a bit of a doomsday prepper and when
he heard that toilet paper was in short supply,

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:46.580
he decided to stock up by buying every roll
the store had.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:49.220
That is, until he heard how much it would
cost.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.780
Not wanting to blow a week’s wages on TP
he didn’t really need, Ted ended up leaving

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:54.890
with only two packs.

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:58.870
So, if it weren’t for that high price, there
wouldn’t have been any toilet paper left

00:02:58.870 --> 00:02:59.870
for you.

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:04.200
You needed it more than Ted, so the pricing
efficiently distributed it to you instead

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:05.200
of him.

00:03:05.200 --> 00:03:09.410
Though it may seem unfair, these economists
argue that pricing is a better system for

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:15.560
determining who gets what than first-come
first-serve or… fisticuffs in the parking lot.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:21.260
In times of scarcity, high prices discourage
hoarding and encourage conservation.

00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:23.420
At least, in theory.

00:03:23.430 --> 00:03:28.080
In the real world, we do see examples of how
gougers exacerbate shortages, like the guy

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:34.110
in Tennessee who famously hoarded 17,000 bottles
of hand sanitizer to sell online.

00:03:34.110 --> 00:03:37.980
He probably wouldn’t have gone from store
to store buying every bottle he could find

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.580
if he knew he couldn’t jack up the price.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:45.330
But economists will claim that even so-called
“entrepreneurs” like him serve a function

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:49.790
in times of crisis, by efficiently moving
goods from places where they have them to

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.180
places where they need them.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.700
For instance, if a hurricane disrupts a town’s
water supply, people from other counties will

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:59.560
happily drive truckloads of bottled water
to the stricken area--if they’re allowed

00:03:59.560 --> 00:04:02.590
to charge prices high enough to make it worth
their while.

00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:07.090
Eventually, the enticement of big profits
will attract so much water that the shortage

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:09.920
will end and prices will normalize.

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.200
Again, in theory.

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:15.720
Economists tend to look at big pictures and
long timeframes.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:20.290
It’s a lot harder for a governor in the
middle of a disaster to tell citizens:

00:04:20.290 --> 00:04:23.850
"I know you can’t afford to feed your family
now, but don’t you see?

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:26.680
At some point down the road the market will
stabilize!”

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:29.500
That… wouldn’t go over well.

00:04:29.500 --> 00:04:34.200
Just because someone can’t afford an inflated
price doesn’t necessarily mean they need

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.940
the resource less than someone who can.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:40.480
Yet such people seem to get left out of these
equations.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:46.310
As economist Angus Deaton said in his response
to the IGM Forum poll: “Efficiency is less

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.630
important than distribution under such…
conditions.”

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:55.030
Whether it’s morality or biology, humans
seem to have an almost universal conviction

00:04:55.030 --> 00:05:00.350
that in times of crisis, everyone should be
pulling together to help each other, not looking

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:02.340
for ways to make a buck.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:05.910
And people who break that code are severely
condemned.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:10.730
Just ask our buddy in Tennessee who received
a ton of hate mail and death threats from

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:17.050
his unwanted infamy, and eventually agreed
to donate his stockpile to avoid prosecution.

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:21.830
Interestingly, this societal judgment is one
of the reasons that economists tend to find

00:05:21.830 --> 00:05:24.960
laws against price gouging unnecessary.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:30.320
In a classic example, if a local hardware
store jacks up the price of snow shovels the

00:05:30.330 --> 00:05:35.260
day after a blizzard, the townspeople might
get angry and punish the store owner by taking

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:37.460
their business elsewhere in the future.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:43.500
So sellers who care about their reputations
already have a powerful motivation not to

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:45.500
seem exploitative or greedy.

00:05:45.500 --> 00:05:50.180
While this motivation may not apply to an
out-of-state entrepreneur hoping to make a

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:56.490
one-time profit by selling overpriced water,
it applies very much to large corporations

00:05:56.490 --> 00:05:59.330
who carefully manage their brands.

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:03.389
After being called out for allowing third
parties to price gouge on their platforms,

00:06:03.389 --> 00:06:08.580
megaretailers like Amazon and Walmart are
now bending over backwards to assure their

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:12.560
customers that they don’t cotton to no dirty
rotten price gougers.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:17.680
And major grocery store chains go to great
lengths to keep their supply chains operating

00:06:17.680 --> 00:06:22.600
during crises so as not to be painted with
the dreaded Scarlet G.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:28.580
As long as big firms keep prices relatively
stable, say economists, individual entrepreneurs

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:34.230
should be allowed to fill in the gaps by charging
whatever people are willing to pay.

00:06:34.230 --> 00:06:38.850
As heartless as economics can sometimes be,
I have to admit it’s kind of comforting

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:42.870
that our beliefs about right and wrong are
at least a part of the equation!

00:06:42.870 --> 00:06:43.870
Right.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:49.040
So, according to economists, price gouging
maybe serves some necessary function, but

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:53.280
the price gougers should still be treated
like greedy jerks?

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:56.600
Seems like a paradox, but I guess that’s
economics!

00:06:56.600 --> 00:06:59.160
Where cold numbers meet warm hearts.

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.000
And that’s our two cents!

